  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 PM CDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nClear skies have returned today as a gentle ridge rests overhead \n(providing weak subsidence). The sky conditions mixed with steady \nsoutherly winds gusting as high as 25-30MPH this afternoon, have \nlead to the quick warmup (highs this afternoon nearing the upper 60s \nto low 70s). Despite the warmer temperatures, overnight lows should \nstill remain on track to fall back down to the upper 30s and 40s. \nTonight, a few models (RRFS, HRRR & NAMNEST) indicate the potential \nfor fog formation, especially across the Platte River Valley. The \nwind directions (south/southeasterly) as well as the presence of a \nfew passing high-level clouds, limit our confidence/concern to a \ndegree tonight for widespread dense fog formation (not ideal \nconditions). In addition, dewpoints (mid 30s to low 40s) may be just \nbe a touch out of reach for temperatures to reach to reach \ncondensation outside of a few more isolated areas.  \n\nBeyond the slight potential for fog Monday morning (30-50% chance), \nsimilar day-time temperatures (mid 60s to low 70s highs) should \nreturn Monday and Tuesday afternoon as the overarching pattern \nremains almost untouched. The winds out of the south will retain, \nthough speeds will be more steady as the surface high pressure \ncenter to the east broadens out (winds 5-10MPH gusting as high as 15-\n25MPH). \n\nThe next notable pattern chance should come around Wednesday as the \nridging across the central U.S. amplifies. This feature will \ncontinue to suppress any precipitation chances through at least \nThursday. In addition, the continuation of the mostly clear skies \nand steady southerly winds should help temperatures lift up into the \n70s to even low 80s by Thursday. Locations near and south of the \nstate line currently look to have a 50-75% chance of seeing highs \nexceed the 70s on Thursday with a 25-50% chance of exceedance for \nthe remainder of south central Nebraska. \n\nBehind this ridge, a building low/trough across the Southwest coast \nwill prepare to makes its assent up to the Northern Plains by the end \nof the week. This disturbance may be responsible for the area's next \nbest precipitation potential Friday night(20-40% chance; increasing \ntowards the west). 30-40% PoPs retain across the day Saturday as a \npotential cold front may make its way through the region. Details \nfor this event remain more wishy-washy as run to run model guidance \nstill remains fairly inconsistent at this time.",
